mdb_env_incr_loadfd on Windows: the end of a pipe ends the input

Why: the Windows read of a page header takes only ERROR_HANDLE_EOF for
the end of the input and turns every other ReadFile failure into -1.
The end of a pipe whose writer closed it is ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE, so
`mdb_load -i` from a pipe loads the whole dump and then fails with
"error -1"; a handle that cannot be read returns -1, not its error; and
a successful read sets rc to the byte count, so a dump cut inside a
page header returns that count as an error code (KNOWN_C_BUGS.md row
66). rlmdb takes the end of a pipe for the end of the input on Windows;
the interop tests feed C 1.0's `mdb_load -i` its dumps through a pipe on
every platform.

What: ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE ends the input as ERROR_HANDLE_EOF does, any
other failure of the header read returns its error code, and a
successful read leaves rc 0 (a short header then ends the load as on
Unix). The read of the rest of a record is unchanged. The line count of
the file is unchanged, so line numbers cited elsewhere stay valid.

Upstream: not reported; not fixed upstream at the pinned commit.

diff --git a/mdb.c b/mdb.c
index 6cf577cb..c8f5feb8 100644
--- a/mdb.c
+++ b/mdb.c
@@ -12003,9 +12003,9 @@ mdb_env_incr_loadfd(MDB_env *env, HANDLE fd)
 
 	for (;;) {
 #ifdef _WIN32
-		rc = ReadFile(fd, buf, sizeof(buf), &rlen, NULL) ? (int)rlen : -1;
-		if (rc == -1 && ErrCode() == ERROR_HANDLE_EOF)
-			rc = 0;
+		if (!ReadFile(fd, buf, sizeof(buf), &rlen, NULL) && (rc = ErrCode()) != ERROR_HANDLE_EOF
+			&& rc != ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE) break;	/* rlmdb's patch 0003 */
+		rc = 0;
 #else
 		rlen = read(fd, buf, sizeof(buf));
 #endif
